# 200: Incremental Feature Restore (beta, the wow-chat roguelike)

## Status
- Created: 2026-03-31 (restore the stashed phase-2 work, one feature at a time)
- Reopened: 2026-10-17, after an audit of the beta build, as the plan for
  bringing beta's whole game back on the current server
- Phase: 2 (The Empty World; the plan reaches into phases 3–9)
- In progress. Reader's version, in order: `docs/profiles/beta-roadmap.md`

## Origin

**March 2026.** The phase-2 work (bot behaviors, the ambush's random
walk, the portal design) sat in a git stash while the build was rebuilt
on a clean branch. This issue listed it in groups and restored it one
group at a time, testing each, with wow-chat-1's scripts kept in
`libs/wow-chat-1/` as the reference. The stash is gone now; what was
restored is in `src/lua-beta/`.

**10 April 2026.** To set up the profile system, every custom feature was
switched off to get a plain baseline (commit e42b59e: "Disabled all
patches and custom features to establish vanilla baseline"). Every beta
script was renamed to `*.lua.disabled`, and the behaviors folder to
`behaviors.disabled/`. Only the ambush came back afterwards, rewritten so
that it scheduled itself. The rest was never switched back on.

**17 October 2026.** The owner asked for an audit "to bring up to the new
quality standard". On learning that treasure and the wandering merchants
and trainers were off: "treasure shouldn't be disabled, nor wandering
merchant trainers". On what beta is for:

> we are going to be able to run multiple at once. When the player
> connects, for each server we have running it shows them in the realm
> select list. Ideally, eventually, we'd run them all at once. They should
> each offer a different experience, and we'll create as many as we can
> think of just because we love the players and want to show more.

And on the assets beta runs on:

> If we can get it running in the beta version of our game that is still
> attached to World of Warcraft (which by the way Blizzard has every access
> to use as much as they'd please including all our assets and machinery
> except for the celebratory "release version" where 100% of the assets
> and components are ours, and any updates made after that)

So beta is a playable realm of its own: the wow-chat survival game on the
game's own content, running beside the others.

## Current Behavior

What beta's server does at boot today (src/lua-beta/, as the engine loads
it; `scripts/test-beta-lua-load` lists it):

- **Runs:** the ambush (monsters sent at every player and bot, on a
  drifting clock, with wow-chat-1's holds restored 2026-10-17), the death
  knights' Ebon Hold exits and auto-completed starting quests, the group
  hearthstone, the Knight's class definition (loaded, but nothing offers
  it), and the extensions (per-object data, bag slots, stack traces).
- **Switched off** (`*.lua.disabled`, or a folder with a leading dot):
  the periodic-events scheduler, treasure chests, travellers (who carry
  the trainers and merchants), ability tomes, death durability, chest
  vulnerability, the custom-class framework, levelling (talent cap), and
  the eleven bot behaviors.
- **The world** after beta's install: creature spawns cleared except
  critters and spirit healers (E012). Trainer spells reworked for 1–20
  (E013, E014), class-selector NPCs and empty loot chests created (E015,
  E016), but nothing places or uses them while travellers, treasure and
  the class framework are off. A new character can't learn a spell.
  Worse, E011, E013 and E014 write their trainer data to `npc_trainer`, a
  table the current server no longer reads (it reads `trainer` and
  `trainer_spell`), so they would do nothing even with trainers present
  (found 2026-10-17; 206's note, 721's first step).
- **The build:** the installed server is from 9 April 2026, older than
  the current upstream pin and the June folder rename; its script link
  points at a folder that no longer exists, so it would boot with no
  custom scripts. Its 11 source patches apply and revert cleanly at the
  current pin (`scripts/test-source-patches <dir> beta`). Reinstalling is
  the owner's build.
- **Shared databases:** beta and release use the same world, characters
  and playerbots databases (136b).

## Intended Behavior

Beta boots into the whole wow-chat loop, on its own realm, beside the
other profiles:

1. an empty world, filled around each player by what comes to them;
2. danger: ambushes on a drifting clock, rares, held while the player rests;
3. reward: treasure chests fed by what players sell, ability tomes;
4. friends: travellers who wander by, among them trainers and merchants,
   who sit when the player sits;
5. companions: bots who behave by the same rules (they are ambushed too);
6. identity: custom classes learned from trainers (502), on the stock
   client where it can be done, with known gaps (tooltips) said plainly;
7. progression and story layers on top (phases 8 and 9).

Each part is switched back on only with a test that shows it working, in
the toy world where it can be (as `scripts/test-ambush` does for the
ambush) and in game where it must be.

## Suggested Implementation Steps

The order and the issues behind each step are in
`docs/profiles/beta-roadmap.md`. In short:

1. **A realm of its own:** own databases and port (136b), then reinstall.
2. **Boot clean and see it:** the boot load test, the ambush tests, an
   in-game run of the ambush (302 closes there), the bot count (open
   question 2).
3. **The scheduler:** periodic_events back on, without starting a second
   ambush clock (the ambush schedules itself now).
4. **Travellers, trainers and merchants** (travel.lua; 503, 504, 505,
   501), so spells can be learned again.
5. **Treasure** (treasure.lua; 402, 404, 407–411), with the sell hook
   (B006) beta already has.
6. **Ability tomes and death durability** (405, 406).
7. **Bot behaviors** one by one (601–616), each with its own check.
8. **Custom classes on the stock client** (502's any-class trainers first,
   then 705/706/709; 707 and 713 need AIO, which is not built).
9. **Progression and story** (801–805, 901–913) as the owner chooses.

